 {"q": "Which statement best captures the difference between the literal sense and literalism?", "options": ["They mean the same thing", "The literal sense is what the author meant, figures included; literalism takes every word at face value whatever the genre", "Literalism is the medieval method and the literal sense is modern", "The literal sense ignores genre"], "answer": 1, "why": "Literalism is a modern approach; the old literal sense depended on genre."},
 {"q": "A reader says: 'Proverbs 22:6 promises that if I bring up my child well, he will never go astray.' What is the best reply from this session?", "options": ["The verse applied only to ancient Israel's kings", "Proverbs is wisdom writing, giving general truths about life rather than guarantees", "The verse is mistranslated", "Only the Hebrew counts"], "answer": 1, "why": "Genre: a proverb states what generally happens, not a promise for every case."},
 {"q": "What did the school of Antioch insist on, against Alexandria?", "options": ["That the Bible should not be translated", "The historical sense: what happened and what the writer meant in his own time", "That only the Gospels are inspired", "That allegory is the only true meaning"], "answer": 1, "why": "Diodore, Theodore and Chrysostom distrusted allegory that floated free of history."}
